# Laser Diffraction Pattern Analysis: Z-axis Rotation Effect

## Overview

This repository contains experimental recordings investigating how the **rotation angle along the z-axis** affects the spatial distribution of laser diffraction patterns — specifically the transition between two distinct phenomena:

- **Fringe line** (z-axis upper region): an elongated streak extending upward from the central spot, resembling a rocket exhaust plume, characteristic of edge/aperture diffraction
- **Satellite spots** (z-axis lower region): discrete secondary maxima arranged in a 2D grid pattern below the central beam

The experiment demonstrates that xy-plane orientation (e.g., square vs. rhombus aperture alignment) has **no significant effect** on pattern distribution; only the z-axis rotation governs which phenomenon dominates and where.

