## Physical Interpretation

The observed fringe-to-satellite transition under z-axis rotation reflects the geometry of diffraction from a 2D aperture or grating:

- **Fringe line** (upper z-axis): When the aperture edge is oriented such that diffraction spreads preferentially along the z-axis, a continuous intensity streak forms above the central beam — analogous to single-slit diffraction producing an elongated sinc² envelope.
- **Satellite spots** (lower z-axis): At complementary orientations, the 2D periodicity of the aperture/grating creates a regular array of constructive interference maxima below the central beam, producing the observed discrete grid of spots.
- **XY-plane independence**: Rotating the aperture within the xy-plane merely reorients the entire pattern azimuthally without changing which diffraction orders are accessible along the z-axis, hence the observed xy-independence.

The transition between regimes as a function of z-axis angle is the central result of this experiment.

